.TH "FEROPROBE" "1" "" "FeroProbe 0.4.0" "FeroProbe Reference Manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
feroprobe - fast, nmap-style network scanner written in Rust
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
feroprobe [Scan Type(s)] [Options] {target specification}
\f[R]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
FeroProbe is a network scanner written natively in Rust.
It finds which hosts on a network are up, which TCP, UDP and SCTP ports
and which IP protocols they answer on, which services and versions run
there, which operating system family they run, and the route to them.
It builds and reads its own packets: it does not use nmap, libpcap or
any external binary, and it ships its own service and OS signatures
rather than nmap\[aq]s databases.
.PP
The command line follows nmap\[aq]s.
Most nmap commands that use only options FeroProbe has run unchanged,
with the same defaults.
.PP
A scan runs in this order:
.IP "1." 3
Targets are read, resolved and filtered by the exclusions.
.IP "2." 3
Host discovery finds the targets that are up (skipped with
\f[V]-Pn\f[R]).
.IP "3." 3
Reverse DNS names the hosts (\f[V]-n\f[R] turns it off).
.IP "4." 3
The port scans run on the hosts that are up: one TCP scan, plus UDP,
SCTP and IP protocol scans if asked for.
.IP "5." 3
Version detection (\f[V]-sV\f[R]) and OS detection (\f[V]-O\f[R]) run on
what the port scans found.
.IP "6." 3
Traceroute (\f[V]--traceroute\f[R]) traces the route to each host.
.IP "7." 3
The reports are written.
.SH OPTIONS SUMMARY
.PP
\f[V]feroprobe --help\f[R] (or \f[V]feroprobe\f[R] with no arguments)
prints a one-page summary of every option, laid out like nmap\[aq]s.
\f[V]feroprobe -V\f[R] prints the version.
.PP
Options can come in any order, before or after the targets.
Single-letter options that take a value accept it with or without a
space (\f[V]-p22\f[R] or \f[V]-p 22\f[R]), except the \f[V]-P\f[R] ping
options and \f[V]-s\f[R] scan types, whose value is always run on
(\f[V]-PS22\f[R], \f[V]-sS\f[R]).
nmap\[aq]s two-letter options \f[V]-iL\f[R], \f[V]-oN\f[R],
\f[V]-oX\f[R], \f[V]-oS\f[R], \f[V]-oG\f[R], \f[V]-oA\f[R] (and
FeroProbe\[aq]s \f[V]-oJ\f[R]) are written exactly as in nmap, as a
separate word followed by their value.
.PP
Durations (\f[V]<time>\f[R]) are in seconds unless they end in
\f[V]ms\f[R], \f[V]s\f[R], \f[V]m\f[R] or \f[V]h\f[R]: \f[V]500ms\f[R],
\f[V]30s\f[R], \f[V]5m\f[R], \f[V]1.5h\f[R].
A bare number means seconds, as in nmap.
Options documented as taking milliseconds (\f[V]--timeout\f[R],
\f[V]--version-timeout\f[R]) take a plain number of milliseconds
instead.
For the RTT timeout options a bare number of 50000 or more is refused
with a hint, as in nmap, since it was almost surely meant as
milliseconds.
.SH TARGET SPECIFICATION
.PP
Everything on the command line that is not an option is a target.
Targets can be mixed freely, IPv4 and IPv6 together.
.PP
