T}
.TE
.PP
A target the system resolver would read as an old-style IPv4 number is
refused rather than scanned: a bare number (\f[V]2222\f[R] would mean
0.0.8.174), fewer than four parts (\f[V]10.1.2\f[R]), octal
(\f[V]010.0.0.1\f[R]) or hexadecimal (\f[V]0x7f000001\f[R]).
A bare number usually means a port was given to the wrong option, and
the error says so (\f[V]-PS22\f[R], not \f[V]-PS 22\f[R]).
.PP
A hostname that does not resolve is skipped with
\f[V]Failed to resolve \[dq]name\[dq].\f[R] and the other targets are
still scanned, as in nmap.
A hostname without an address of the wanted family is skipped with a
hint to add (or drop) \f[V]-6\f[R].
If no target is left, FeroProbe warns that 0 hosts were scanned.
.PP
A single run expands to at most 2\[ha]20 hosts.
.SS \f[V]-iL <inputfile>\f[R] (long form \f[V]--input-file\f[R])
.PP
Read more targets from a file.
Targets are separated by spaces, tabs or newlines, and \f[V]#\f[R]
starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.
Any target form above works.
\f[V]-\f[R] reads standard input.
Targets from the file are added to those on the command line.
.SS \f[V]--exclude <host1[,host2],...>\f[R]
.PP
Leave these hosts out of the scan.
Takes a comma-separated list of addresses, CIDR blocks (of any size),
ranges or hostnames.
Hostnames are resolved, and every address they resolve to is excluded.
.SS \f[V]--excludefile <file>\f[R]
.PP
Leave out the hosts listed in a file, written like an \f[V]-iL\f[R]
file.
\f[V]-iL -\f[R] and \f[V]--excludefile -\f[R] cannot both read standard
input.
.SS \f[V]-6\f[R]
.PP
Scan the IPv6 address of each hostname target instead of its IPv4
address.
Address targets are always scanned as given, so \f[V]-6\f[R] is not
needed for IPv6 addresses.
.SS \f[V]--randomize-hosts\f[R]
.PP
Scan the targets in random order instead of the order given.
.SH HOST DISCOVERY
.PP
Before port scanning, FeroProbe finds out which targets are up and scans
only those.
A host is up as soon as any probe draws an answer; a host that answers
none is reported as down and is not scanned.
The probes are nmap\[aq]s.
.SS Default probes
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Situation
T}@T{
Probes sent to each target
T}
_
T{
Raw sockets available (root or \f[V]CAP_NET_RAW\f[R] on Linux)
T}@T{
ICMP echo request, TCP SYN to port 443, TCP ACK to port 80, ICMP
timestamp request (IPv4 only).
For IPv6 targets the echo is ICMPv6
T}
T{
No raw sockets
T}@T{
TCP connects to ports 80 and 443, plus an ICMP echo request through an
unprivileged ping socket where the operating system offers one (not on
Windows)
T}
.TE
.PP
A TCP connect that completes, or is refused with a reset, proves the
host exists.
nmap without privileges sends no ICMP at all; FeroProbe uses the
unprivileged ping socket that Linux and macOS offer.
.SS Local network: ARP and neighbour discovery
.PP
As root on Linux, targets on a directly connected Ethernet network are
asked for their MAC address with ARP (IPv4) or neighbour discovery
(IPv6) first, as nmap does.
Their answer, or its absence, decides them alone, and the ping probes go
only to the other targets.
The answer also gives the host\[aq]s MAC address and its vendor (see
\f[V]--oui-file\f[R] under Miscellaneous options).
The reason shown is \f[V]arp-response\f[R] or \f[V]nd-response\f[R].
.SS This machine
.PP
As root, a target that is this machine (a loopback address, or on Linux
one of its own addresses) is marked up without any probe, with the
reason \f[V]localhost-response\f[R], as nmap does.
Without raw sockets it is probed like any other target.
Traffic to your own address goes over the loopback interface, so a
capture on the Ethernet interface does not show it.
.SS Discovery options
.PP
Giving any \f[V]-P\f[R] option other than \f[V]-Pn\f[R] replaces the
default probes: only the probes you ask for are sent.
The options can be repeated and combined, for example
\f[V]-PE -PP -PS22,443 -PA80\f[R].
Port lists are written like \f[V]-p\f[R] lists and run on without a
space; \f[V]-PS 22\f[R] is an error because \f[V]22\f[R] would be read
as a target.
A probe type the process cannot send (for example \f[V]-PP\f[R] without
raw sockets) is skipped, and the other probes decide.
.SS \f[V]-sn\f[R] (no port scan)
.PP
Only find out which hosts are up, then report them; no port scan.
Cannot be combined with other \f[V]-s\f[R] scan types,
\f[V]--scanflags\f[R], \f[V]-O\f[R] or \f[V]-A\f[R].
It can be combined with \f[V]--traceroute\f[R].
.SS \f[V]-Pn\f[R] (no ping)
.PP
Skip host discovery and treat every target as up: every target is port
scanned, and every target is reverse-resolved.
\f[V]-P0\f[R] is accepted as an old spelling.
Cannot be combined with other \f[V]-P\f[R] options.
\f[V]-sn -Pn\f[R] would do nothing and is refused unless
\f[V]--traceroute\f[R] is given (trace only).
.PP
As in nmap, targets on a local Ethernet network still get one ARP (IPv4)
or neighbour discovery (IPv6) request when FeroProbe runs with raw
sockets, unless \f[V]--disable-arp-ping\f[R] is given: the answer gives
the report\[aq]s MAC address and latency and times the port scan\[aq]s
first probes.
Targets that do not answer are scanned all the same.
Other targets show the latency the port scan measured.
.SS \f[V]-PS[portlist]\f[R] (TCP SYN ping)
.PP
Send a TCP SYN to each port (default port 80).
A SYN-ACK or a reset proves the host is up.
With raw sockets on Linux the SYN is a bare raw packet; otherwise it is
a full TCP connect, as in nmap.
.SS \f[V]-PA[portlist]\f[R] (TCP ACK ping)
.PP
Send a bare TCP ACK to each port (default port 80).
A live host answers with a reset.
Needs raw sockets on Linux; otherwise the ports get a TCP connect
instead, as in nmap.
.SS \f[V]-PU[portlist]\f[R] (UDP ping)
.PP
Send a UDP datagram to each port (default port 40125, which is unlikely
to be open).
A reply, or an ICMP port unreachable, proves the host is up.
Datagrams to well-known ports carry that service\[aq]s probe payload
(see UDP scan).
.SS \f[V]-PE\f[R] (ICMP echo)
.PP
Send an ICMP echo request (ICMPv6 for IPv6 targets).
Needs raw sockets, or an unprivileged ping socket where the OS has one.
.SS \f[V]-PP\f[R] (ICMP timestamp)
.PP
Send an ICMP timestamp request.
IPv4 only, needs raw sockets.
.SS \f[V]-PM\f[R] (ICMP address mask)
.PP
Send an ICMP address mask request.
IPv4 only, needs raw sockets.
Few hosts answer it today.
.SS \f[V]--disable-arp-ping\f[R], \f[V]--send-ip\f[R]
.PP
Do not use ARP or neighbour discovery: local targets get the ping probes
like remote ones.
The two names do the same thing; \f[V]--send-ip\f[R] is nmap\[aq]s.
.PP
nmap\[aq]s \f[V]-PO\f[R] (IP protocol ping), \f[V]-PY\f[R] (SCTP ping)
and \f[V]-PR\f[R] (ARP ping only) are not supported and are refused with
an error.
ARP happens on its own as described above.
.PP
\f[V]--exclude-ports\f[R] also removes its ports from the \f[V]-PS\f[R],
\f[V]-PA\f[R] and \f[V]-PU\f[R] probes and from the default TCP probes,
as nmap does.
If that leaves a TCP or UDP ping type with no ports, it is skipped with
a warning.
.SH REVERSE DNS
.PP
By default FeroProbe looks up the name of every host that is up (of
every target with \f[V]-Pn\f[R]).
The name is shown as \f[V]Scan report for name (address)\f[R], written
as a \f[V]type=\[dq]PTR\[dq]\f[R] hostname in XML, and included in
grepable and JSON output.
When a target was given by hostname, that name is shown, and a different
reverse name is printed as \f[V]rDNS record for <address>: <name>\f[R].
.SS \f[V]-n\f[R]
.PP
Never do reverse DNS lookups.
Cannot be combined with \f[V]-R\f[R].
.SS \f[V]-R\f[R]
.PP
Look up the names of all targets, including those that are down.
.SS \f[V]--dns-servers <serv1[,serv2],...>\f[R]
.PP
Ask these DNS servers directly instead of using the system resolver.
Each is an IP address, optionally with a port
(\f[V]192.0.2.53:5353\f[R], \f[V][2001:db8::53]:53\f[R]); the default
port is 53.
FeroProbe uses its own resolver for these queries.
Target hostnames are still resolved by the system resolver.
