T}
.TE
.PP
\f[V]-sO\f[R] can run together with a TCP, UDP or SCTP scan.
.SS Version detection (\f[V]-sV\f[R])
.PP
Listed with the scan types because it is given as \f[V]-s\f[R] option;
see Service and version detection.
.SH PORT SPECIFICATION AND SCAN ORDER
.PP
Without \f[V]-p\f[R], \f[V]-F\f[R] or \f[V]--top-ports\f[R], FeroProbe
scans the 1000 most common TCP ports and the 1000 most common UDP ports,
as nmap does, the 50 most common SCTP ports, and, with \f[V]-sO\f[R],
all 256 IP protocols.
.PP
The TCP and UDP lists are nmap\[aq]s: the same ports in the same order
as nmap\[aq]s \f[V]--top-ports\f[R], ranked by the frequencies in
nmap\[aq]s \f[V]nmap-services\f[R] (nmap 7.94).
The default scan, \f[V]-F\f[R] (the first 100) and
\f[V]--top-ports N\f[R] (the first \f[V]N\f[R]) cover the same ports as
nmap\[aq]s.
Only the port numbers and their order are taken; the names FeroProbe
prints come from the IANA registry.
.PP
\f[V]-p-\f[R] scans all 65535.
.SS \f[V]-p <port ranges>\f[R], \f[V]--ports <port ranges>\f[R]
.PP
Scan only these ports.
A comma-separated list of single ports (\f[V]22\f[R]), ranges
(\f[V]1-1024\f[R]), open-ended ranges (\f[V]-1024\f[R],
\f[V]60000-\f[R]) and \f[V]-\f[R] for all of 1-65535 (\f[V]-p-\f[R]).
Port 0 cannot be scanned.
A protocol prefix applies to the ports after it, up to the next prefix:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Prefix
T}@T{
Applies to
T}
_
T{
\f[V]T:\f[R]
T}@T{
TCP ports
T}
T{
\f[V]U:\f[R]
T}@T{
UDP ports
T}
T{
\f[V]S:\f[R]
T}@T{
SCTP ports
T}
T{
\f[V]P:\f[R]
T}@T{
IP protocol numbers (0-255) for \f[V]-sO\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
Values before any prefix apply to every scan that runs:
\f[V]-sS -sU -p 22,U:53,T:80\f[R] scans TCP 22 and 80 and UDP 22 and 53.
FeroProbe warns, like nmap, when the prefixes do not match the scans
(\f[V]-sS -p U:53\f[R] warns that \f[V]U:\f[R] needs \f[V]-sU\f[R]), and
a scan type left with no ports is skipped with a warning rather than
failing.
Cannot be combined with \f[V]--top-ports\f[R] or \f[V]-F\f[R].
.SS \f[V]--top-ports <n>\f[R]
.PP
Scan the \f[V]n\f[R] most common ports of each protocol scanned, from
the ranked lists above.
Past the end of a list (1000 TCP, 1000 UDP, 52 SCTP ports) the remaining
ports are added in ascending order.
Without it the defaults above apply.
.SS \f[V]-F\f[R] (fast mode)
.PP
Scan the 100 most common ports of each protocol scanned.
Cannot be combined with \f[V]-p\f[R] or \f[V]--top-ports\f[R].
.SS \f[V]--exclude-ports <port ranges>\f[R]
.PP
Leave these ports out of the scan.
Written like \f[V]-p\f[R], prefixes included; with \f[V]-sO\f[R],
\f[V]P:\f[R] numbers are left out of the protocol list.
As in nmap, the excluded ports are also removed from the discovery
probes (see Host discovery).
.SS \f[V]-r\f[R] (don\[aq]t randomize ports)
.PP
Probe ports in numerical order.
By default the order is shuffled, as in nmap.
Reports are always sorted.
.SH SERVICE AND VERSION DETECTION
.SS \f[V]-sV\f[R]
.PP
After the port scan, find out which service and version run on each open
port.
It runs on the hosts found up, together with any TCP scan (the default
one if none was given) and \f[V]-sU\f[R].
Cannot be combined with \f[V]-sn\f[R].
It works without privileges.
.PP
The probes and the rules that read the replies come from FeroProbe\[aq]s
service rules file.
nmap\[aq]s probe database is not used, because its licence does not
allow redistributing it.
Each probe has a rarity from 0 to 9: the higher, the less likely it is
to help.
For each open TCP port, FeroProbe tries, until a rule matches:
.IP "1." 3
On the TLS probe\[aq]s ports (443, 993, 8443 and similar), a TLS
handshake first, then the probes inside the tunnel.
The certificate\[aq]s subject name is shown with the version
(\f[V]cert CN: example.org\f[R]).
.IP "2." 3
The probes meant for that port, whatever the intensity: HTTP on 80 and
8080, Redis on 6379, SMB on 445, and so on.
.IP "3." 3
The \f[V]null\f[R] probe: connect and listen, since SSH, FTP, SMTP,
POP3, IMAP, MySQL, VNC and others speak first.
.IP "4." 3
Every other probe whose rarity is at most the intensity, the most common
first, as nmap does.
.IP "5." 3
A TLS handshake, when the port stayed silent or answered with a TLS
alert.
.PP
A \f[B]soft match\f[R] names the service but not the product (a
\f[V]+OK\f[R] greeting is POP3, but whose?).
Probing then goes on only with the probes that can tell more about that
service, as in nmap (with every probe at intensity 9).
Open UDP ports get the UDP probes the same way, without the listen and
TLS steps.
Like nmap, UDP ports the scan left \f[V]open|filtered\f[R] are probed
too, and one that answers becomes \f[V]open\f[R] (reason
\f[V]udp-response\f[R]).
.PP
Each probe opens a new connection and waits up to
\f[V]--version-timeout\f[R] for a reply, so a silent port costs one wait
per probe tried: at the default intensity about 15 probes.
\f[V]--version-light\f[R] makes such ports much quicker.
.PP
Protocols with their own probes: HTTP, RTSP, SIP, SMB, RDP, Redis, LDAP,
ONC RPC, DNS (TCP and UDP), Microsoft SQL Server, PostgreSQL, SOCKS5,
memcached (TCP and UDP), AMQP, MQTT, MongoDB, ZooKeeper, X11, Java RMI,
AJP, XMPP, PPTP, Erlang Port Mapper, Cassandra, beanstalkd, git, SNMP,
NetBIOS name service, NTP, SSDP, IPMI and OpenVPN.
Services recognized from what they send first: SSH, FTP, SMTP, POP3,
IMAP, MySQL and MariaDB, VNC, telnet, rsync, NNTP, IRC, NATS, ActiveMQ,
Subversion, dictd and ident.
FeroProbe sends no probes for industrial protocols (Modbus, S7 and the
like): a version probe is a request the device has to parse.
.PP
The SERVICE column shows the service (\f[V]ssl/http\f[R] for HTTP inside
TLS) and a VERSION column is added.
Below the ports, a \f[V]Service Info\f[R] line collects what the
services gave away about the host, as in nmap: host names, the operating
system, the device type and operating-system CPEs
(\f[V]Service Info: Host: mail.example.org; OS: Linux; CPE: cpe:/o:linux:linux_kernel\f[R]).
The XML report carries every CPE as \f[V]<cpe>\f[R] elements of
\f[V]<service>\f[R] and the \f[V]hostname\f[R], \f[V]ostype\f[R] and
\f[V]devicetype\f[R] attributes; the JSON report has the same fields.
.PP
A reply no rule recognizes is shown with a \f[V]?\f[R] after the usual
service name for the port and, at intensity 7 or more (as in nmap),
printed under \[dq]Unrecognized service replies\[dq] in normal output
and as \f[V]servicefp\f[R] in XML: the starting point for writing a new
rule.
.SS \f[V]--version-intensity <level>\f[R]
.PP
How many probes to try, from 0 to 9; default 7, as in nmap.
A probe is sent when its rarity is at most the level, or when it is
meant for the port.
At 0, an open port only gets the probes meant for it and the
\f[V]null\f[R] probe.
At 9 every probe is tried, and soft matches no longer narrow the probes.
The last of \f[V]--version-intensity\f[R], \f[V]--version-light\f[R] and
\f[V]--version-all\f[R] wins, as in nmap.
Without \f[V]-sV\f[R] (or \f[V]-A\f[R]) the option does nothing, and
FeroProbe warns.
.SS \f[V]--version-light\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]--version-intensity 2\f[R]: the probes meant for the port, the
\f[V]null\f[R] probe, HTTP, the generic line probe and TLS.
.SS \f[V]--version-all\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]--version-intensity 9\f[R]: every probe.
.SS \f[V]--version-trace\f[R]
.PP
Print what version detection does to standard output: each probe sent,
each reply (escaped, up to 120 bytes), and each match with the rule that
made it, in the layout of nmap\[aq]s version trace.
When a JSON, XML or grepable report goes to standard output
(\f[V]-o json\f[R], \f[V]-oX -\f[R], ...), the trace goes to standard
error instead, so the report stays readable:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Service scan sending probe null to 10.0.0.5:22 (tcp)
Service scan got 32 bytes in reply to probe null from 10.0.0.5:22 (tcp): \[dq]SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_9.6p1 Debian-4\[rs]r\[rs]n\[dq]
Service scan hard match (Probe null matched with built-in rules line 332): 10.0.0.5:22 is ssh.  Version: |OpenSSH|9.6p1|Debian-4; protocol 2.0|
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The rule is named by its line in the rules file (or as a built-in parser
or the built-in HTTP header reader), which makes the trace the tool for
writing and debugging rules.
nmap\[aq]s version trace also prints its network library\[aq]s debug
lines; FeroProbe prints only the lines above.
.SS \f[V]--service-rules <file>\f[R]
.PP
Read the probes and rules from \f[V]<file>\f[R] instead of the built-in
ones.
The file replaces the built-in rules completely, so start from a copy of
\f[V]crates/feroprobe-core/data/service-rules.txt\f[R].
A mistake in the file stops the scan before anything is sent, naming the
line.
nmap\[aq]s \f[V]--versiondb\f[R] takes nmap\[aq]s own format; FeroProbe
does not read that format and says so if given an
\f[V]nmap-service-probes\f[R] file.
.SS \f[V]--version-timeout <ms>\f[R]
.PP
How long to wait for each service reply, in milliseconds.
Default 3000.
The timing templates do not change it.
.SS Service rules file
.PP
The rules file is plain text in FeroProbe\[aq]s own format.
It starts with the line \f[V]feroprobe-service-rules 1\f[R].
\f[V]#\f[R] starts a comment line.
Directives start at the beginning of a line; their attributes follow on
indented lines.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
feroprobe-service-rules 1

probe null tcp
  rarity 0

probe redis tcp
  rarity 6
  ports 6379,6380
  send \[dq]*2\[rs]r\[rs]n$4\[rs]r\[rs]nINFO\[rs]r\[rs]n$6\[rs]r\[rs]nserver\[rs]r\[rs]n\[dq]

match redis \[ha]\[rs]$\[rs]d+\[rs]r\[rs]n.*?redis_version:([\[rs]d.]+)
  product Redis key-value store
  version ${1}
  cpe cpe:/a:redis:redis:${1}

softmatch pop3 \[ha]\[rs]+OK\[rs]x20
  probe null
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[V]probe NAME tcp|udp\f[R] defines a probe:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
