.SH OS DETECTION
.SS \f[V]-O\f[R]
.PP
Enable OS detection.
For every host where the port scan found an open TCP port, FeroProbe
sends a small set of probes, reads how the host\[aq]s TCP/IP stack
answers, and matches that fingerprint against its own OS database, which
names OS versions where the probes can tell them apart (for example
Linux 4.19 - 6.x against Linux 3.x - 4.18, or Windows 10 - 11 against
Windows 7).
.PP
One try sends a host at most 9 probes, all to ports and protocols the
command already probes:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
T{
Probe
T}@T{
Sent to
T}@T{
What it is
T}
_
T{
\f[V]s1\f[R]-\f[V]s4\f[R]
T}@T{
the open TCP port
T}@T{
four SYNs with different TCP option sets, 100 ms apart
T}
T{
\f[V]e\f[R]
T}@T{
the open TCP port
T}@T{
a SYN with ECE and CWR (asks for ECN)
T}
T{
\f[V]a\f[R]
T}@T{
the open TCP port
T}@T{
an ACK outside any connection
T}
T{
\f[V]c1\f[R], \f[V]c2\f[R]
T}@T{
a closed TCP port, if the scan found one
T}@T{
a SYN and an ACK
T}
T{
\f[V]ie\f[R]
T}@T{
the host, only if the command\[aq]s host discovery sends ICMP echo
requests (the default as root, or \f[V]-PE\f[R])
T}@T{
an ICMP or ICMPv6 echo request with the \f[V]feroprobe\f[R] payload
T}
.TE
.PP
The fingerprint records, per probe, whether it was answered, the TCP
flags, window, option layout and \[dq]don\[aq]t fragment\[dq] bit of the
answer, and across probes the initial TTL (IPv6: hop limit), the window
scale, the IP ID sequence (IPv4), how the TCP timestamp clock runs, and
whether the host answers ECN.
Each signature in the database lists the values it expects; its accuracy
is the share of its tests (weighted) that match.
A signature that matches every test the fingerprint has is a perfect
match, reported as the OS:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Device type: general purpose
Running: Linux 4.X|5.X|6.X
OS CPE: cpe:/o:linux:linux_kernel:4 cpe:/o:linux:linux_kernel:5 cpe:/o:linux:linux_kernel:6
OS details: Linux 4.19 - 6.x
Network Distance: 1 hop
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Without a perfect match FeroProbe prints
\f[V]No exact OS matches for host\f[R] and the fingerprint (lines
starting with \f[V]OS:\f[R]), so it can be added to the database, see
docs/os-fingerprints.md.
Signatures that match at 85% or better are shown as
\f[V]Aggressive OS guesses\f[R] with \f[V]--osscan-guess\f[R], or when
the conditions were not ideal (for example no closed port), as in nmap.
With \f[V]-v\f[R] the IP ID sequence class is shown
(\f[V]IP ID Sequence Generation: All zeros\f[R]); with \f[V]-vv\f[R] the
fingerprint is shown even for a perfect match.
The network distance is 0 for this machine, 1 for a host whose MAC
address discovery found, and otherwise counted from the TTL.
.PP
As in nmap, a host without a perfect match is tried again, up to
\f[V]--max-os-tries\f[R] times (default 5), or at most twice when the
conditions make a match unlikely: no closed TCP port, a host more than
five hops away, or a \f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] above 500 ms.
A host with an open but no closed TCP port gets the warning
\f[V]OSScan results may be unreliable because we could not find at least 1 open and 1 closed port\f[R].
A host without an open TCP port gets no OS probes at all (nmap would
probe its closed port and random closed ports outside the scan, see
Differences from nmap).
.PP
The XML report has nmap\[aq]s \f[V]<os>\f[R] element
(\f[V]<portused>\f[R], \f[V]<osmatch>\f[R] with \f[V]<osclass>\f[R] and
\f[V]<cpe>\f[R], \f[V]<osfingerprint>\f[R]), then \f[V]<distance>\f[R]
and \f[V]<ipidsequence>\f[R]; the grepable report lists perfect matches
after \f[V]OS:\f[R]; the JSON report has the full result under
\f[V]os\f[R].
.PP
\f[V]-O\f[R] needs raw sockets (Linux, root or \f[V]CAP_NET_RAW\f[R])
and a port scan; it is an error without raw sockets and cannot be
combined with \f[V]-sn\f[R].
If no port scan is left to run (every scan type lost its ports), OS
detection is skipped with a warning.
IPv4 and IPv6 hosts are both covered; the answers are read from a packet
capture, which shows the IP header of both.
The TCP probes deliberately carry no FeroProbe window marker, since they
must look like a real client\[aq]s SYNs; the echo request carries the
usual \f[V]feroprobe\f[R] payload.
\f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] and \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] show each probe\[aq]s
TCP options (\f[V]<mss 1460,sackOK,timestamp ...>\f[R]).
With \f[V]ics_protection\f[R] on, \f[V]-O\f[R] is refused (see ICS
protection).
.SS \f[V]--osscan-limit\f[R]
.PP
Only run OS detection against hosts where the scan found at least one
open and one closed (or unfiltered) TCP port, as in nmap.
Other hosts are skipped (with \f[V]-v\f[R], a line says so), which saves
time on many hosts.
No effect without \f[V]-O\f[R] or \f[V]-A\f[R] (a warning says so).
.SS \f[V]--osscan-guess\f[R], \f[V]--fuzzy\f[R]
.PP
When no signature matches perfectly, also print the near matches (85% or
better) as \f[V]Aggressive OS guesses: Linux 4.19 - 6.x (93%), ...\f[R]
and the \f[V]Running (JUST GUESSING)\f[R] line, as in nmap.
No effect without \f[V]-O\f[R] or \f[V]-A\f[R].
.SS \f[V]--max-os-tries <tries>\f[R]
.PP
How many tries a host without a perfect match gets (default 5, at least
1).
Each try sends the probe set again.
A lower value is faster and gentler on hosts the database does not know;
it does not raise the limit of two tries for hosts without a closed
port.
No effect without \f[V]-O\f[R] or \f[V]-A\f[R].
.SH TRACEROUTE AND AGGRESSIVE MODE
.SS \f[V]--traceroute\f[R]
.PP
After the port scan, trace the route to each host that is up.
FeroProbe sends TCP SYN probes with TTL (IPv6: hop limit) 1 to 30 all at
once, to a TCP port the scan found open on that host, else one it found
closed, else a fallback port the command names: the first port of the
TCP scan; with \f[V]-sn\f[R], the first TCP port given with
\f[V]-p\f[R]; else the first TCP port of host discovery (\f[V]-PS\f[R],
\f[V]-PA\f[R], or the default 443 as root).
Traceroute never probes a port outside the scan: when the command probes
no TCP port at all (for example \f[V]-sU -Pn\f[R]), hosts without an
open or closed TCP port are not traced and a warning says why (with
\f[V]-sn\f[R], name the port with \f[V]-p\f[R]).
A directly connected host needs no port, since it gets no probes (see
below).
Routers answer the probes that expire with ICMP time exceeded, and the
first probe that reaches the host ends the route.
A hop that never answers is shown as \f[V]...\f[R].
Needs raw sockets.
With \f[V]-Pn\f[R] every target is traced, even one that never answered.
Hosts given up on by \f[V]--host-timeout\f[R] are not traced.
.PP
As in nmap, this machine itself (a loopback address or one of its own)
is not traced at all, and a host on a directly connected network gets no
probes: its route is the host alone, at hop 1, with the latency the scan
measured, under a plain \f[V]TRACEROUTE\f[R] header (in XML, a
\f[V]<trace>\f[R] element without a port).
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
TRACEROUTE (using port 443/tcp)
HOP RTT        ADDRESS
1   0.29 ms    192.168.1.1
2   ...
3   9.84 ms    203.0.113.5
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To trace without a port scan, combine it with \f[V]-sn\f[R], and with
\f[V]-sn -Pn\f[R] to skip host discovery too:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
sudo feroprobe -sn --traceroute example.org
sudo feroprobe -sn -Pn --traceroute -p 443 example.org
\f[R]
.fi
.SS \f[V]-A\f[R] (aggressive detection)
.PP
The same as \f[V]-sV -O --traceroute\f[R].
Without raw sockets only \f[V]-sV\f[R] runs, rather than failing, as in
nmap.
Unlike nmap, which drops the other parts silently, FeroProbe prints a
warning naming them:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Warning: -A without root or CAP_NET_RAW skips OS detection (-O) and traceroute (--traceroute), which send raw packets; only version detection (-sV) runs. Run as root, or grant CAP_NET_RAW, to include them
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
nmap\[aq]s \f[V]-A\f[R] also runs its default scripts (\f[V]-sC\f[R]);
FeroProbe has no scripts.
Cannot be combined with \f[V]-sn\f[R].
.SH TIMING AND PERFORMANCE
.SS Timing templates (\f[V]-T<0-5>\f[R])
.PP
\f[V]-T0\f[R] to \f[V]-T5\f[R], or \f[V]-T\f[R] with a name
(\f[V]-T paranoid\f[R] ...
\f[V]-T insane\f[R]), pick a bundle of timing settings, as in nmap.
\f[V]-T3\f[R] (normal) is the default.
Any of \f[V]--timeout\f[R], the RTT timeout options,
\f[V]--retries\f[R], \f[V]-c\f[R]/\f[V]--concurrency\f[R],
\f[V]--min-parallelism\f[R], \f[V]--max-rate\f[R],
\f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] and \f[V]--max-scan-delay\f[R] given explicitly
overrides the template\[aq]s value for that setting, so
\f[V]-T4 --max-rate 2000\f[R] is aggressive with a lower packet rate.
Given more than once, the last \f[V]-T\f[R] wins, as in nmap.
.PP
